<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gallia Belgica</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Belgica</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gallia</a> Belgica was a Roman province in north-eastern Gaul, organised under Augustus from the lands of the Belgae conquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a>. Its capital was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Durocortorum</a>, modern Reims, and it covered parts of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, Luxembourg and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a> districts were later separated as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germania-inferior">Germania Inferior</a> and Superior.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gallia-belgica.jpg" alt="Gallia Belgica" width="320" height="267" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Milenioscuro · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roman_Empire_-_Belgica_(125_AD).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Celtic peoples practised their religion across much of Iron Age Europe, from Ireland and Britain through Gaul and <b>Belgica</b> to southern Germany, Bohemia and Celtiberian Iberia.</blockquote><a href="/religion/celtic-religion">Celtic religion · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Votive coins scattered over the floor of the mithraeum at Pons Sarravi (Sarrebourg) in <b>Gallia Belgica</b> run from Gallienus to Theodosius I: when the temple was destroyed, Christians apparently regarded the coins as polluted and left them behind.</blockquote><a href="/religion/mithraism">Mithraism · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Marching west into <b>Belgica</b> with Gepids, Ostrogoths, Alans and other vassals, Attila captured Metz, killing most of its people and burning the town; he also took Strasbourg.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-7">7 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Their name matches the Menapii of <b>Belgic Gaul</b>, which has led to suggestions of migration or trading links across the sea.</blockquote><a href="/state/manapii">Manapii</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Rome Durocortorum became capital of the province of <b>Gallia Belgica</b>, with monuments such as the Porte de Mars.</blockquote><a href="/state/remi">Remi</a></li><li><blockquote>Caesar was told that within living memory their king Diviciacus had been the most powerful ruler in Gaul, holding sway over much of <b>Belgic Gaul</b> and over Britain.</blockquote><a href="/state/suessiones">Suessiones</a></li></ul></section>
